  hreflang={[
    { lang: 'en', url: `${site}/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'de', url: `${site}/de/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'es', url: `${site}/es/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'ro', url: `${site}/ro/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'uk', url: `${site}/uk/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'ru', url: `${site}/ru/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'fr', url: `${site}/fr/${slug}` },
    { lang: 'pt', url: `${site}/pt/${slug}` },
  ]}
  articleMeta={{ datePublished: '2026-09-09' }}
>

  <section class="art-hero">
    <div class="art-orb art-orb--1"></div>
    <div class="art-orb art-orb--2"></div>
    <div class="art-grid"></div>
    <div class="art-hero__inner container">
      <div class="art-meta">
        <span class="art-tag">Stripchat</span>
        <span class="art-tag art-tag--green">Platform Update</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="art-title">Stripchat's Plasma Streaming: <span class="art-grad">Phone Instead of a Rig</span></h1>
      <p class="art-lead">Stripchat now ships its own mobile broadcasting app, Plasma Streaming — tap and go live from a phone, no capture card or OBS scene to configure. It's not a replacement for a real setup, but it changes what "streaming today" requires. Here's what it does and where it actually fits.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="art-body container">
    <div class="art-layout">
      <aside class="art-toc">
        <p class="art-toc__title">Contents</p>
        <ol class="art-toc__list">
          <li><a href="#what-is-it">What Plasma Streaming Actually Is</a></li>
          <li><a href="#setup">Getting It Running</a></li>
          <li><a href="#tradeoffs">What You Gain, What You Give Up vs. OBS</a></li>
          <li><a href="#when-to-use">When Mobile Streaming Actually Makes Sense</a></li>
          <li><a href="#for-studios">For Studios: A Roster That Streams From Phones</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
        <a href="#cta" class="art-toc__cta">Get help →</a>
      </aside>

      <main class="art-main">

      <p class="art-intro">Most streaming advice still assumes a desk, a capture card, and an OBS scene collection. Plasma Streaming skips all of it — it's Stripchat's own app, free on iOS and Android, built to get a phone camera live with none of that setup. That's a real option now, not a workaround, and it's worth knowing exactly what it trades away before you build a routine around it.</p>
      <p class="card-date" style="margin:0 0 1.75rem;">EWOhub Editorial Team · Published Sep 9, 2026</p>

      <section class="section" id="what-is-it">
        <h2>What Plasma Streaming Actually Is</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>Plasma Streaming is a dedicated mobile app built by Stripchat for broadcasting straight from a phone — open it, tap to go live, no scene setup or hardware in between. It supports quick camera switching, adjustable aspect ratios, streaming quality up to 1080p, and Picture-in-Picture so a show keeps running while you check messages in another app.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">It isn't a browser workaround — Stripchat built and maintains it specifically because phone-camera video through a browser tab has always looked and behaved worse than a dedicated app. Plasma is the platform's own answer to that gap, not a third-party tool.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="setup">
        <h2>Getting It Running</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>Open the Broadcasting Center from your phone's browser, find the "Download the app" panel, and follow the on-screen instructions — the app links straight back to your existing account, so there's no separate signup. Once it's installed, going live is a single tap from the app itself; there's no scene, source, or bitrate menu to get through first.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">That simplicity is the entire pitch. Everything OBS makes you configure by hand — resolution, camera source, audio input — Plasma picks sensible defaults for automatically. You lose control over those defaults in exchange for not needing to touch them.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="tradeoffs">
        <h2>What You Gain, What You Give Up vs. OBS</h2>

        <div class="two-col-box">
          <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--good">
            <h3>What a phone + Plasma gets you</h3>
            <ul class="checklist">
              <li>Live in minutes with zero setup — no scenes, no capture card, no dedicated streaming PC</li>
              <li>Genuine portability: stream from anywhere with a signal, not just one fixed desk</li>
              <li>Picture-in-Picture keeps a show running while you handle chat, notes, or messages elsewhere</li>
              <li>One less point of failure — no dropped capture-card connection or crashed OBS scene mid-show</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="two-col-box__item two-col-box__item--bad">
            <h3>What a real rig still gives you that a phone doesn't</h3>
            <ul class="checklist">
              <li>Custom lighting, multiple camera angles, and overlays — a phone camera is one fixed lens</li>
              <li>Fine control over bitrate and resolution when your connection is unstable</li>
              <li>Chat overlays, alerts, and goal widgets layered into the scene itself</li>
              <li>Consistent framing — handheld or propped-up phone shots drift in ways a mounted rig doesn't</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="when-to-use">
        <h2>When Mobile Streaming Actually Makes Sense</h2>

        <div class="reason-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <div class="reason-num">01</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Traveling or between setups</h3>
            <p>A canceled show because you're away from your desk is a canceled show — Plasma turns that into a lower-production one instead of a missed one. Consistency in your schedule (see our <a href="/best-streaming-times-by-region">best streaming times by region →</a>) matters more to followers than any single session's polish.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">02</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Testing a second daily slot without buying more gear</h3>
            <p>Adding a short extra session at a different hour is easy to try with a phone and expensive to try by duplicating a full rig. If a mobile slot performs, it's evidence worth acting on either way — build it out properly or keep it mobile because it's working as-is.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">03</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Recovering from equipment failure</h3>
            <p>A dead capture card or a crashed OBS install used to mean canceling the day. Plasma is a real fallback that keeps a show live at reduced production quality instead of not live at all — worth having installed before you need it, not after.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">04</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Where it's the wrong call: your main daily show</h3>
            <p>Viewers who follow a model for a specific look, lighting, and set notice the downgrade. Plasma is a genuine tool for gaps and edge cases — treating it as a permanent replacement for a proper setup on your primary sessions trades production quality for convenience you didn't actually need.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="for-studios">
        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h2>For Studios: A Roster That Streams From Phones</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">A free, no-setup mobile option changes the studio math in a specific way: it lowers the equipment floor for onboarding a new model (see our <a href="/stripchat-first-14-days-guide">first-14-days guide →</a>) and gives traveling or between-desks models a way to hit their schedule without missing sessions. It is not, on its own, a reason to skip investing in proper rigs for models who are streaming regularly — quality differences between phone and rig setups are visible, and a studio's core roster competing on Popular rankings still needs the production edge. Use Plasma deliberately: as onboarding-gap coverage, travel continuity, and equipment-failure backup, not as a standing substitute for studio-provided gear.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-section section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Does Plasma Streaming cost anything?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>It's a free download for iOS and Android, linked directly to your existing Stripchat account through the Broadcasting Center — there's no separate purchase or subscription to use it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Does streaming from a phone hurt your ranking compared to a desktop setup?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>The ranking signals themselves — viewer count, retention, chat engagement, tip frequency — don't check what app you're streaming from (see our <a href="/how-cam-algorithm-ranks-rooms">ranking signals breakdown →</a>). What can hurt you indirectly is lower video quality or a less engaging setup costing you retention, which is a production issue, not a platform penalty.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Can you use Plasma alongside a regular desktop setup, or is it one or the other?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Both, and that's the more common pattern — a desktop rig for regular scheduled sessions, Plasma installed and ready for travel days, equipment failures, or a quick extra session that wouldn't otherwise happen at all.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Trending right now</h2>
        <p style="color:var(--fg-muted);margin:-0.5rem 0 1.25rem;">The promotion service our clients ask about most this month.</p>
        <a href="/stripchat-promo" class="glass-card" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;overflow:hidden;padding:0;text-decoration:none;">
